#- Callables: defer and feed
#-
#- Aurora has no functions. "defer" takes a scope and delays it, producing a
#- value that can be invoked later; "feed(n)" reads the nth value applied to the
#- running scope.
#-
#- There is no signature, no arity and no parameter list: executing is applying
#- a vector of values to a scope. The scope never learns how many it received —
#- feed(n) normalizes the index, so it always answers.
#-
#- Run: aurora run examples/callables.ar
#-
#- Output:
#-   [0 0 0 0 0 0 0 3]
#-   [0 0 0 0 0 0 0 30]
#-   [0 0 0 0 0 0 0 12]
#-   [0 0 0 0 0 0 0 42]
#-   [0 0 0 0 0 0 0 10]

ident sum = defer {
  feed(0) + feed(1);
};

print sum(1, 2);
print sum(10, 20);

#- Naming the fed values makes a scope easier to read.
ident area = defer {
  ident width = feed(0);
  ident height = feed(1);
  width * height;
};

print area(3, 4);

#- A deferred scope is a value: it can be bound and passed around.
ident double = defer { feed(0) * 2; };
print double(21);

#- The scope is blind to how many values arrive. Extra ones are simply unread.
print double(5, 99, 100);
